module mp-superior-court-civil.civil

// Briefs on a motion: the memorandum supporting or opposing a motion may
// not exceed 25 pages, and a reply 15 pages, except by prior permission of
// the court (NMI R. Civ. P. 7).
/// A memorandum of law supporting or opposing a motion: at most 25 pages
/// except by prior permission of the court (NMI R. Civ. P. 7).
limit motion-memorandum { pages <= 25 cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 7" } cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 7"
/// A reply memorandum: at most 15 pages except by prior permission of the
/// court (NMI R. Civ. P. 7).
limit reply-memorandum { pages <= 15 cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 7" } cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 7"

/// A memorandum supporting or opposing a written motion is filed and
/// served (NMI R. Civ. P. 6(c), 7).
event MotionMemorandumFiled() paper motion-memorandum
/// A reply memorandum is filed and served (NMI R. Civ. P. 6(c), 7).
event ReplyMemorandumFiled() paper reply-memorandum

start complaint-filed

state complaint-filed: "Complaint filed" by applicant cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 3"
{
  note "A civil action is commenced by filing a complaint with the Superior Court (NMI R. Civ. P. 3). Periods in this package are stated, not counted: Rule 6(a) excludes the trigger day, includes the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday or legal holiday, and excludes intermediate Saturdays, Sundays and legal holidays from a period of 10 days or less unless it is expressed in calendar days; legal holidays include those declared under 1 CMC § 311. Three days are added after service by mail, by leaving it with the clerk or by other Rule 5(b) means (NMI R. Civ. P. 6). The filing fee is not stated in the vendored rules. The earlier outline named no leading decision for this step."
  edge-complaint-filed-served "Summons and complaint served" -> answer-due {
    authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 4"
  }
  edge-complaint-filed-unserved "Defendant not served within 120 days" -> service-review {
    authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 4"
  }
}

decision service-review: "Good cause for late service shown?" by court cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 4"
{
  note "If a defendant is not served within 120 days after the complaint is filed, the court, on motion or on its own after notice to the plaintiff, must dismiss the action without prejudice or direct that service be made within a specific time; on good cause it must extend the time, and an extension is freely given when justice so requires. Late service is not grounds for dismissal once the defendant has been served, and Rule 4(m) does not apply to service in a foreign country under Rule 4(f) or (j)(1). The federal rule allows 90 days (NMI R. Civ. P. 4, note [4])."
  edge-service-review-cause "Time extended: service made" -> answer-due {
    authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 4"
  }
  edge-service-review-dismissed "Dismissed without prejudice" -> dismissed-unserved {
    authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 4"
  }
}

terminal dismissed-unserved: "Dismissed without prejudice for want of service" outcome [loss, dismissal] cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 4"

decision answer-due: "Answer, motion or default" by office cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 12"
{
  note "Unless another time is specified by the rule or by Commonwealth law, a defendant serves an answer within 21 days after being served with the summons and complaint or, having timely waived service under Rule 4(d), within 60 days after the request for a waiver was sent (90 days if sent outside any jurisdiction of the United States); the Commonwealth, its agencies and officers or employees sued in an official capacity answer within 30 days after service on the Attorney General, and those sued individually for acts on the Commonwealth's behalf within 30 days after service on them or on the Attorney General, whichever is later. A Rule 12 motion alters these periods: if the court denies it or postpones it to trial, the responsive pleading is due within 14 days after notice of the court's action (NMI R. Civ. P. 12). The clerk may enter once, on consent, an order extending the time to plead by 10 days (NMI R. Civ. P. 77)."
  edge-answer-due-default "No answer or defense" -> default-entered {
    authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 55"
  }
  edge-answer-due-motion "Rule 12(b) motion" -> pleading-motion {
    authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 12"
  }
  edge-answer-due-answer "Answer served" -> discovery { authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 12" }
}

decision pleading-motion: "Motion to dismiss decided" by court cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 12"
{
  note "A written motion made before judgment is filed and served no later than 30 days before the hearing unless it may be heard ex parte or a different period is fixed by rule or order; an opposition is filed and served not later than 9 days after service of the motion and a reply not later than 6 days after service of the opposition (NMI R. Civ. P. 6). The supporting or opposing memorandum may not exceed 25 pages and a reply 15 pages without prior permission; if the court decides to rule without a hearing it allows the parties 2 days to object and request one (NMI R. Civ. P. 7). The earlier outline named no Commonwealth pleading-standard decision."
  edge-pleading-motion-granted "Granted" -> dismissed { authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 12" }
  edge-pleading-motion-amend "Leave to amend" -> amend { authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 15" }
  edge-pleading-motion-denied "Denied: answer within 14 days of notice" -> discovery {
    authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 12"
  }
}

state amend: "Amended pleading" by applicant cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 15"
{
  note "A party may amend once as a matter of course within 21 days after serving the pleading or, if a responsive pleading is required, within 21 days after service of the responsive pleading or of a Rule 12(b), (e) or (f) motion, whichever is earlier; otherwise only with the opposing party's written consent or the court's leave, freely given when justice so requires. A response to an amended pleading is due within the time remaining on the original or within 14 days after service of the amendment, whichever is later (NMI R. Civ. P. 15)."
  edge-amend-tested "Amended pleading tested again" -> pleading-motion {
    authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 12"
  }
}

terminal dismissed: "Dismissed on the pleadings" outcome [win, dismissal] cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 12"
{
  note "Outcome from the defending side. Whether the dismissal is with or without prejudice is the court's order; appeal runs from the entry of judgment (NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a))."
}

decision default-entered: "Default" by court cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 55"
{
  note "When a party against whom affirmative relief is sought has failed to plead or otherwise defend, and that is shown by affidavit or otherwise, the clerk must enter its default. The clerk enters judgment for a sum certain against a defendant defaulted for not appearing who is neither a minor nor incompetent; in other cases the party applies to the court, and a party that has appeared is served written notice of the application at least 7 days before the hearing unless the court orders otherwise. The court may set aside an entry of default for good cause and a default judgment under Rule 60(b) (NMI R. Civ. P. 55). The earlier outline named no set-aside decision."
  edge-default-entered-judgment "Default judgment entered" -> default-judgment {
    authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 55"
  }
  edge-default-entered-set-aside "Default set aside for good cause" -> discovery {
    authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 55"
  }
}

terminal default-judgment: "Default judgment" outcome [loss] cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 55"
{
  note "Outcome from the defending side."
}

state discovery: "Disclosure, discovery and pretrial" by applicant cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 26"
{
  note "Initial disclosures are made within 14 days after the parties' Rule 26(f) conference unless a stipulation or order sets another time; a party joined later discloses within 30 days after being served or joined. Expert disclosures are made at least 90 days before the trial date (rebuttal within 30 days after the other party's), pretrial disclosures at least 30 days before trial, and objections to them within 14 days after they are made (NMI R. Civ. P. 26). The scheduling conference is conducted within 90 days after the complaint is filed or within 45 days of the last pleading permitted by Rule 7, whichever comes last, subject to any other date the court sets (NMI R. Civ. P. 16). A jury is demanded by serving a written demand not later than 60 days after the last pleading is filed and paying the jury fee by the date the demand is filed; another party may demand other issues within 14 days after service, and a proper demand may be withdrawn, with the parties' consent, no later than 45 days before trial (NMI R. Civ. P. 38)."
  edge-discovery-sj "Summary judgment motion" -> summary-judgment {
    authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 56"
  }
  edge-discovery-trial "No dispositive motion" -> trial { authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 38" }
  edge-discovery-settled "Voluntary dismissal" -> settled { authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 41" }
}

decision summary-judgment: "Summary judgment decided" by court cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 56"
{
  note "Unless the court orders otherwise, a party may move for summary judgment at any time until 30 days after the close of all discovery (NMI R. Civ. P. 56). The motion follows the Rule 6 motion periods: filed and served no later than 30 days before the hearing, opposition within 9 days after service, reply within 6 days after service of the opposition (NMI R. Civ. P. 6). The earlier outline named no Commonwealth summary judgment decision."
  edge-summary-judgment-granted "Granted" -> judgment-entered { authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 56" }
  edge-summary-judgment-denied "Denied" -> trial { authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 56" }
}

state trial: "Trial by jury or by the court" by court cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 38"
{
  note "Issues triable of right by a jury are so tried on a timely demand (NMI R. Civ. P. 38). In a nonjury trial, the prevailing party prepares draft findings of fact and conclusions of law within 10 days after the announcement of the decision unless the court orders otherwise (NMI R. Civ. P. 58)."
  edge-trial-judgment "Verdict or decision" -> post-trial { authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 58" }
  edge-trial-settled "Settled before verdict" -> settled { authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 41" }
}

decision post-trial: "Post-trial motions" by applicant cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 59"
{
  note "A renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law is filed no later than 10 days after entry of judgment, or after the jury was discharged on an issue not decided by a verdict (NMI R. Civ. P. 50(b)); a motion to amend findings no later than 10 days after entry of judgment (NMI R. Civ. P. 52(b)); a motion for a new trial no later than 20 days after entry of judgment, with opposing affidavits within 14 days after service, and the court may order a new trial on its own within the same 20 days; a motion to alter or amend no later than 10 days after entry (NMI R. Civ. P. 59(b), (d), (e)). The federal rules allow 28 days for each (NMI R. Civ. P. 50, 52, 59, notes). The court may not extend these times except as the rules state (NMI R. Civ. P. 6). A post-judgment motion and its notice of hearing are filed and served not later than 14 calendar days before the hearing, the opposition within 7 calendar days after service and the reply within 2 calendar days (NMI R. Civ. P. 6). A timely motion under Rule 50(b), 52(b), 54(d)(2), 59(e) or 59, or under Rule 60 filed within 28 days after judgment, restarts the time to appeal from the order disposing of the last of them (NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a))."
  edge-post-trial-resolved "Motions resolved or none filed" -> judgment-entered {
    authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 58"
  }
}

decision judgment-entered: "Judgment entered" by office cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 58"
{
  note "Where a judgment requires settlement as to form, the prevailing party prepares and serves a draft within 10 days after the announcement of the decision, the other parties state approval or disapproval within five days, and the clerk submits the drafts to the judge after 15 days (NMI R. Civ. P. 58). The clerk may tax costs on 7 days' notice, reviewable on motion served within the next 7 days; a motion for attorney's fees is filed no later than 14 days after entry of judgment unless a statute or order provides otherwise (NMI R. Civ. P. 54). The earlier outline named no finality decision."
  edge-judgment-entered-appeal "Notice of appeal within 30 days" -> appeal-taken {
    authority cite "NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)"
  }
  edge-judgment-entered-final "No appeal" -> final-no-appeal { authority cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 58" }
}

terminal appeal-taken: "Appeal taken: continue in the NMI Supreme Court package" outcome [appeal] cite "NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)"
{
  note "The notice of appeal is filed with the Superior Court clerk within 30 days after entry of the judgment or order appealed from; another party may appeal within 14 days after the first notice or within the time otherwise prescribed, whichever ends later (NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(1), (3)). Hand-off to lit/mp-supreme (mp-supreme-court-civil)."
}

terminal final-no-appeal: "Final: no appeal" outcome [final] cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 58"

terminal settled: "Settled: voluntary dismissal" outcome [settlement] cite "NMI R. Civ. P. 41"
{
  note "The plaintiff may dismiss without a court order by a notice of dismissal before the opposing party serves an answer or a summary judgment motion, or by a stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared; the dismissal is without prejudice unless stated otherwise, but a second notice of dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits (NMI R. Civ. P. 41)."
}
